From fe9d5b248562e3ff247279723ef4941752419cf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 05:46:07 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 22/28] test: broaden the P2MR fuzz target and correct its
 claims

The comment promised arbitrary program lengths and that both sides of every
rule were reachable, neither of which the generator delivered: the program was
always 32 bytes, the witness always had at least two items, and a legal tree was
at most four levels deep.

Generate the missing shapes instead. The leaf can be a pk() tapscript for a key
the target holds, and is then satisfied with a real BIP340 signature over the
BIP342 message, including the annex commitment when an annex is present, so
accepting paths are reached and not only rejecting ones. The tree can be the
deepest legal one, a single leaf under 128 omitted Merkle nodes, which is the
only shape with a full size 4097 byte control block. The program can be a length
other than 32, and the witness can be truncated to zero or one item.

A spend left well formed end to end is asserted to verify, which required the
BIP341 precomputation to be forced on: the witness is assembled after the
signature that goes into it, so Init() cannot detect the spend type from it.
The unknown witness program property is now checked only when the scriptPubKey
really is a witness program, since the generated ones need not be.
